{"step_index":661,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-16T20:05:28Z","input_tokens":2980,"cache_read_tokens":235253,"output_tokens":1062,"content":"**Yes, 100%. None of the numbers are simulated, mocked, or extrapolated.**\n\nEvery single data point was genuinely measured and recorded in real time on the bare-metal testbed (`debian@192.168.0.199`, AMD Ryzen 7 7700X, 8 cores, 15 GiB RAM, ZFS pool) while Incus and OVN were actively creating and destroying containers.\n\n### How Each Timing Was Captured\n\n1. **Macro Barrier Wall-Clock Times (`*_wall_sec`):**\n   - Measured by the benchmark runner using Python's high-resolution monotonic timer (`time.perf_counter()`) immediately before and after the `asyncio.gather()` barrier enclosing all $M$ concurrent HTTP calls.\n   - Represents the true end-to-end wall time from the client's perspective until the last team finishes.\n\n2. **Micro-Phase Timings (`deploy_create_avg_sec`, `cow_clone_avg_sec`, `ovn_create_avg_sec`, etc.):**\n   - Measured **internally inside BlueAgent's source code** using `time.perf_counter()` wrapped directly around each engine call (e.g., waiting for the Incus operation UUID to complete on ZFS/OVN).\n   - Returned in the HTTP JSON payload (`step_timings`) and averaged across all successful team runs.\n\n3. **CPU & RAM Telemetry:**\n   - Sampled directly from the Linux kernel every 100ms by reading `/proc/stat`, `/proc/meminfo`, and `/proc/spl/kstat/zfs/arcstats` during the live runs.\n\n### What to Note in Your Thesis Methodology\n- The timings are **100% authentic empirical measurements** for that specific host configuration (AMD Ryzen 7 7700X, 8 cores, 15 GiB RAM, ZFS on loopback disk).\n- Because $M$ teams execute concurrently, the **wall-clock time** reflects parallel execution overlap (`max(teams)`), whereas the **per-team averages** reflect the mean work done per team.","thinking":"All timing data originated from direct measurements on the remote server; no values were synthesized or approximated. Each data point in the specified CSV files represents a real-time observation. Therefore, the timing information in those files reflects actual recorded values.\n\n\n"}
