{"step_index":663,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-16T20:07:54Z","input_tokens":3206,"cache_read_tokens":236228,"output_tokens":1538,"content":"### 1. Definitions\n\n* **`deploy_create_cold` (`deploy_create_avg_sec`):**\n  * **What it does:** Provisions a new container instance from a **base image** in the image catalog (`source: {\"type\": \"image\", \"alias\": image}`).\n  * **Under the hood:** Incus calls ZFS to instantiate a brand-new rootfs dataset (`blueagent-zfs/containers/c-...`) from the base image's read-only template, initializes instance metadata, and writes the record to the Incus internal database.\n  * **Measured window:** Elapsed time from sending `POST /1.0/instances` (image source) until Incus signals the operation is complete.\n\n* **`verify_cow_clone` (`cow_clone_avg_sec`):**\n  * **What it does:** Creates an ephemeral replica from an **already running challenge container** (`source: {\"type\": \"copy\", \"source\": source_container, \"instance_only\": true}`).\n  * **Under the hood:** Incus takes an instantaneous ZFS snapshot of the live container (`@snapshot`) and creates a cloned dataset (`blueagent-zfs/containers/verify-...`) via **ZFS Copy-on-Write block pointers** ($O(1)$ metadata transaction).\n  * **Measured window:** Elapsed time from sending `POST /1.0/instances` (copy source) until Incus signals the operation is complete.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Are They Comparable?\n\n**Yes—they represent the two competing architectural strategies for state isolation in a Cyber Range:**\n\n1. **Functional Equivalence:**\n   Both operations accomplish the exact same prerequisite: **instantiating an isolated container rootfs on disk so it can be booted and verified.**\n\n2. **The Thesis Research Question:**\n   When an automated verification engine evaluates a challenge, it has two choices:\n   * **Strategy A (Traditional / Naive Re-deployment):** Spin up fresh replica containers from the challenge image (`deploy_create`).\n   * **Strategy B (BlueAgent Ephemeral Sandboxing):** Clone the live running containers via ZFS CoW snapshots (`verify_cow_clone`).\n   Comparing them quantitatively proves the speedup of Strategy B (up to **$3.6\\times$ faster**, $14\\text{s}$ vs. $50\\text{s}$ at scale).\n\n3. **The Semantic Advantage (Important for your Defense):**\n   They are not only comparable, but CoW cloning is **semantically superior**:\n   * `deploy_create` resets the service back to its original image state, **losing** any patches or configuration files the team deployed.\n   * `verify_cow_clone` clones the live container **in-situ**, verifying the team's actual patched system without risking production downtime.","thinking":"`deploy_create_cold` appears to measure the time taken to create an instance. The relevant code snippet indicates this time is captured within a `create_instance` call. This duration likely represents cold instance creation, though more context is needed to definitively confirm this interpretation.\n\nNow to define `verify_cow_clone`.\nThe search continues for `verify_cow_clone` definition, which could provide insights into how these two timing metrics relate, if at all.\n\n\n`deploy_create_sec` measures cold instance creation time using image-based instantiation via `POST /instances`. It involves cloning/unpacking an image snapshot into a ZFS dataset and configuring the instance. `cow_clone_sec` measures the time to create a copy-on-write clone from a running container using a ZFS snapshot and `POST /instances` with a source of `copy`. The two metrics are thus comparable, representing distinct methods for provisioning verification sandboxes.\n\n\n"}
