n and performance.\n\n\nThe `thesis_evaluation_chapter.md` artifact has been created in `/home/davidel/.gemini/antigravity/brain/335ea41a-df46-4ffa-bb4c-eefc5a5248cd`. This artifact provides a comprehensive analysis of the multi-tenant CTF platform, including detailed methodology, experimental results, and a deep dive into identified bottlenecks and performance optimizations. The artifact includes graphs and analysis of lifecycle events.\n\n\nDetailed system specifications are necessary for reproducibility, including the OS, CPU, RAM, disk type, virtualization software, and SDN. Two container images, Alpine and Ubuntu-heavy, representing lightweight and heavyweight workloads, are key to understanding performance implications. The six-step barrier synchronization architecture, involving team provisioning, challenge deployment, and verification phases, is critical for understanding the experimental setup's design. The primary metric is CoW clone speedup versus cold deployment, quantified through Alpine image deployments.\n\n\nCoW significantly accelerates deployment, with a 3.44x speedup observed for Ubuntu.  CoW's O(1) property is confirmed; large image size differences have minimal clone time impact. Memory density analysis shows linear memory scaling, although Ubuntu images consume more per container. Host RAM utilization reaches 59% with 100 Ubuntu containers; swap usage remained zero across all tests. CPU load remains relatively low, indicating I/O is the primary bottleneck.\n\n\nI/O wait averaged 31-44% during deployment and cloning, suggesting storage I/O is the primary bottleneck. Network provisioning per team shows linear scaling at 0.35-0.40s. A race condition in dynamic IP allocation was resolved using static IPs during clone attachment.\n\nThe thesis structure is being drafted: introducing evaluation objectives (scalability, clone efficacy, memory density, CPU/IO, SDN overhead), detailing methodology (hardware, workloads, telemetry, test harness), and presenting performance results (CoW vs. cold, latency breakdown, memory footprint, lifecycle telemetry).\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_to_file","args":{"ArtifactMetadata":"{\"RequestFeedback\":false,\"Summary\":\"Comprehensive academic thesis evaluation chapter summarizing all empirical experiments, methodology, hardware testbed, telemetry engine, ZFS CoW performance, linear memory scaling, host resource bounds, and architectural insights.\",\"UserFacing\":true}","CodeContent":"\"# Chapter: Empirical Performance and Scalability Evaluation of BlueAgent\\n\\n## Abstract\\nModern cybersecurity competitions—specifically Attack/Defense and King-of-the-Hill Capture The Flag (CTF) events—demand real-time verification of team submissions within deterministic, isolated execution environments. Traditional approaches either deploy full virtual machines (incurring prohibitive cold-start latencies and hypervisor overhead) or rely on shared container namespaces (compromising multi-tenant isolation and risking cross-team interference). \\n\\nThis chapter presents an empirical evaluation of **BlueAgent**, an autonomous multi-tenant CTF orchestration platform that combines Open Virtual Network (OVN) Software-Defined Networking (SDN) with ZFS-backed Copy-on-Write (CoW) container sandboxing. We evaluate BlueAgent across two contrasting image profiles—a lightweight Alpine Linux distribution ($3.21\\\\text{ MiB}$) and a production-grade \\\"Heavy\\\" Ubuntu system ($1.5\\\\text{ GiB}$ rootfs with `systemd`, PostgreSQL 16, and Nginx)—scaling up to 100 concurrent container instances across 10 independent tenant networks. \\n\\nOur findings demonstrate that ZFS snapshot cloning achieves a **$3.44\\\\times$ to $3.60\\\\times$ speedup** over cold container deployment while exhibiting near-perfect filesystem size invariance ($O(1)$ block-pointer metadata cloning). Furthermore, memory consumption scales strictly linearly ($14.9\\\\text{ MB}$/container for Alpine, $52.7\\\\text{ MB}$/container for Ubuntu), enabling 100 concurrent services to operate reliably on a single $16\\\\text{ GiB}$ host with $0\\\\text{ MB}$ swap paging and $<21\\\\%$ average CPU utilization.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Evaluation Objectives & Research Questions\\n\\nThe empirical evaluation of BlueAgent was designed to answer five central systems and architectural research questions:\\n\\n* **RQ1 (Verification Latency & Speedup):** Does instantaneous ZFS snapshot cloning provide a statistically significant latency reduction compared to cold container deployment when setting up \n<truncated 16819 bytes>","Description":"\"Complete academic thesis evaluation chapter in structured Markdown with rigorous analysis, equations, tables, and telemetry insights\"","Overwrite":"true","TargetFile":"\"/home/davidel/.gemini/antigravity/brain/335ea41a-df46-4ffa-bb4c-eefc5a5248cd/thesis_evaluation_chapter.md\"","toolAction":"\"Writing thesis evaluation chapter\"","toolSummary":"\"Write thesis chapter artifact\""}}],"truncated_fields":["tool_calls"]}
